Investigate elements of the local economy such as:
- wealth-creation, wealth-distribution, and poverty;
- businesses;
- natural resources and industries;
- agriculture;
- public sector;
- environment and tourism;
- waterways;
- infrastructure and transportation; and,
- availability of capital, cooperatives.

Research historical or contemporary provincial politicians and their contributions to Saskatchewan public policy decision-making, such as:
- Amédée Forget;
- Walter Scott;
- Louise McKinney;
- Tommy Douglas; and,
- Brad Wall.

Examine unique Saskatchewan political, social or economic initiatives involving Indigenous communities such as:
- Office of the Treaty Commissioner;
- Office of the Commissioner of the Indigenous Languages;
- MN-S Child and Family Services;
- ITEP/SUNTEP;
- Whitecap Dakota Nation;
- Cowessess;
- First Nations University of Canada; and,
- Gabriel Dumont Institute.
